+\title{Drag on finite circular cylinders towed through water}
+\author{[Author names and affiliations to be added]}
+\date{\today}
+\begin{document}
+\maketitle
+
+\begin{abstract}
+Mean drag was measured for three nominally smooth circular cylinders of diameter 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm, towed through a water tank at speeds from 0.2 to 1.1 m s$^{-1}$. Each cylinder had a reported submerged length of 0.60 m. The existing processing gives mean drag coefficients of 1.144, 1.153, and 1.178 for the 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm cylinders, respectively, over nominal Reynolds numbers of approximately $5.1\times10^3$ to $5.6\times10^4$. The coefficients are broadly similar across the tested sizes and speeds. These values are preliminary: the current notebook uses water properties at 20 $^\circ$C despite logged temperatures of 13.7--17.6 $^\circ$C, and no blockage correction or uncertainty budget has yet been applied. Details needed for a publication-ready account of the rig, mounting, calibration uncertainty, and repeatability are identified below.
+\end{abstract}
+
+\section{Introduction}
+The drag on a circular cylinder depends on Reynolds number and on finite-span and facility effects. In the subcritical range, measurements on smooth circular cylinders commonly show a comparatively weak variation in mean drag coefficient with Reynolds number, although geometry, free-stream turbulence, and end conditions affect the measured value \cite{wieselsberger1923,achenbach1968}. A tow tank provides a direct way to measure the integrated load on a finite body in water, but interpretation requires a clearly specified reference area and accounting for the tank boundaries and supports.
+
+This study reports tow-tank measurements for three cylinder diameters over a common range of towing speeds. The immediate objectives are to document the existing data set, compare the resulting drag coefficients across diameter and speed, and establish a reproducible analysis record. The present draft treats the calculated coefficients as preliminary pending correction of water properties and a fuller assessment of systematic effects.
+
+\section{Methods}
+\subsection{Facility and test articles}
+Measurements were made in a tow tank reported in the project notes to be 0.9 m wide and 0.75 m deep. Three smooth circular cylinders with nominal diameters $D=25.4$, 38.1, and 50.8 mm were tested. The submerged length was reported as $L=0.60$ m. The run log identifies each diameter as 1, 1.5, or 2 in, respectively. The cylinder orientation, end treatment, support geometry, surface roughness, and exact definition of submerged length were not recorded in the files reviewed and must be supplied by the authors.
+
+\subsection{Runs and force measurement}
+Each diameter was tested at ten nominal speeds from 0.2 to 1.1 m s$^{-1}$, in 0.1 m s$^{-1}$ increments. The 1 in cylinder was measured on 14 October 2025, the 2 in cylinder on 3 November 2025, and the 1.5 in cylinder on 12 March 2026. The run log records water temperatures of 16.9--17.6 $^\circ$C, 15.0--15.6 $^\circ$C, and 13.7--14.3 $^\circ$C for these sessions, respectively. Each run's load-cell voltage was tared using the mean voltage for $t<1$ s. The mean force and its within-window standard deviation were calculated over $3<t<8$ s. The two earlier sessions use a load-cell sensitivity obtained from the September 2025 calibration; March data use the reported March sensitivity of 0.0213 V N$^{-1}$. The March DAQ used a semicolon-delimited format.
+
+No replicate runs at a given speed are present in the run log. Run 17 (2 in cylinder, 0.8 m s$^{-1}$) is flagged in the notes because its zero appeared offset after the run. It is retained in the current plot and diameter mean; its exclusion should be evaluated after inspection of the raw trace and baseline drift.
+
+\subsection{Dimensionless quantities}
+The drag coefficient was calculated as
+\begin{equation}
+ C_D = \frac{F_D}{\tfrac12\rho U^2 D L},
+ \label{eq:cd}
+\end{equation}
+where $F_D$ is the tare-corrected mean force, $U$ is the nominal tow speed, $D$ is cylinder diameter, $L$ is submerged length, and $\rho$ is water density. The reference area is the projected side area $DL$. The Reynolds number was calculated as
+\begin{equation}
+ Re_D = \frac{UD}{\nu},
+ \label{eq:re}
+\end{equation}
+where $\nu$ is kinematic viscosity. The existing notebook sets $\rho=998$ kg m$^{-3}$ and $\nu=1.004\times10^{-6}$ m$^2$ s$^{-1}$ (approximately 20 $^\circ$C) for every run. Accordingly, reported Reynolds numbers and coefficients reproduce the current analysis, rather than a final temperature-corrected reduction.
+
+\section{Results}
+The existing analysis contains 30 runs across the three diameters. The nominal Reynolds number spans approximately $5.1\times10^3$ to $5.6\times10^4$. Figure~\ref{fig:cd-re} shows the run-wise coefficients calculated with the fixed properties described above. The diameter-averaged values are $C_D=1.144$ for the 25.4 mm cylinder, 1.153 for the 38.1 mm cylinder, and 1.178 for the 50.8 mm cylinder. These averages are descriptive only; they do not account for run-to-run repeatability or measurement uncertainty. The flagged run 17 produces $C_D=1.357$, higher than the other 2 in results, and warrants review before drawing conclusions about diameter dependence.
+
+\begin{figure}[ht]
+ \centering
+ \includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{../figures/cd_vs_re.png}
+ \caption{Run-wise drag coefficient versus Reynolds number using the existing analysis, which assumes $\nu=1.004\times10^{-6}$ m$^2$ s$^{-1}$ for all runs. The flagged 2 in run at 0.8 m s$^{-1}$ is included.}
+ \label{fig:cd-re}
+\end{figure}
+
+\begin{table}[ht]
+\centering
+\caption{Summary of the existing, uncorrected analysis.}
+\label{tab:summary}
+\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
+\toprule
+Cylinder & $D$ (mm) & Runs & $C_D$ mean & Speed range (m s$^{-1}$) \\
+\midrule
+1 in & 25.4 & 10 & 1.144 & 0.2--1.1 \\
+1.5 in & 38.1 & 10 & 1.153 & 0.2--1.1 \\
+2 in & 50.8 & 10 & 1.178 & 0.2--1.1 \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+\section{Discussion}
+The three diameter means are close, with a total spread of about 3\% relative to their overall mean. The plotted points are consistent with a broadly similar coefficient over the tested range, but the measurements do not yet establish a universal or Reynolds-independent coefficient. Each speed and diameter appears to have only one run, and run 17 is an identified potential outlier. Further, cylinder diameter is confounded with measurement date and water temperature: each size was tested in a separate session, and the largest cylinder was not tested under the same load-cell installation as the March cylinder.
+
+The tank cross section is 0.675 m$^2$. If the cylinders span the 0.9 m tank width and are aligned across the flow, the nominal frontal blockage based on $DL$ is approximately 2.3--4.5\%, before support blockage; this estimate depends on geometry not documented in the available notes. Blockage, wall proximity, free-surface effects, end effects, support drag, and the method used to establish towing speed may all affect the inferred coefficient. These need to be documented and assessed before comparison with canonical infinite-cylinder data.
+
+The reduction should be updated to use the logged temperature for each run when selecting $\rho$ and $\nu$. A complete uncertainty analysis should combine calibration-fit uncertainty, baseline drift, force fluctuations over the selected interval, tow-speed uncertainty, dimensional uncertainty, and uncertainty in submerged length. The within-window force standard deviation reported by the processing scripts is not a substitute for independent repeatability. In particular, the raw trace and tare history for run 17 should be checked and the exclusion decision reported transparently.
+
+\section{Conclusions}
+Tow-tank drag measurements were assembled for 30 runs on three finite circular cylinders, spanning nominal Reynolds numbers of roughly $5\times10^3$--$6\times10^4$. The current processing yields mean coefficients from 1.144 to 1.178 across diameters. The data provide a useful preliminary comparison, while temperature-dependent property corrections, a documented uncertainty budget, assessment of run 17, and facility and support corrections remain necessary for a defensible final result.
+
+\section*{Data and code availability}
+Raw records, calibration files, run metadata, processing scripts, and the analysis notebook are included in this project. The analysis and figure are managed as project artifacts with Calkit. A final release should include a versioned repository identifier and an archived data record.
